      "math_academy_id": "e-10707",
      "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=2,$ then what is the value of \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}\\,?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=f(2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}=\\sqrt{2^2-2\\cdot 2} = 0.\n$$",
